WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:04.873
Welcome to Ritym PulseStage MIDI Studio. This guided walkthrough uses

2
00:00:04.873 --> 00:00:09.902
verified captures of the actual application. You can test the keyboard

3
00:00:09.902 --> 00:00:14.475
and reference pedal on this computer, without a physical instrument.

4
00:00:15.675 --> 00:00:20.435
For this demo, choose PulseStage Keyboard as the input, and PulseStage

5
00:00:20.435 --> 00:00:26.061
Pedal Test as the output. The keyboard sends notes into the input monitor.

6
00:00:26.061 --> 00:00:30.821
The command station sends settings out to the reference pedal. There

7
00:00:30.821 --> 00:00:33.850
is no automatic forwarding between these paths.

8
00:00:35.050 --> 00:00:41.026
Click a key on the integrated piano. Pressing sends a note on message.

9
00:00:41.026 --> 00:00:46.542
Releasing sends note off. The keyboard has its own channel and velocity

10
00:00:46.542 --> 00:00:50.679
settings. This demonstrates MIDI messages; the keyboard itself does

11
00:00:50.679 --> 00:00:52.058
not generate audio.

12
00:00:53.258 --> 00:00:58.213
Open Event monitor to inspect incoming notes. Look for a received note

13
00:00:58.213 --> 00:01:03.581
on and note off pair. RX counts incoming messages, while TX counts commands

14
00:01:03.581 --> 00:01:08.948
sent by PulseStage. A sent message alone does not prove that the receiving

15
00:01:08.948 --> 00:01:10.600
device acted on it.

16
00:01:11.800 --> 00:01:17.018
Return to Control room. Choose Control change, then controller eleven,

17
00:01:17.018 --> 00:01:22.235
Expression. Enter twenty and click Send command. The reference receiver

18
00:01:22.235 --> 00:01:27.975
shows twenty. Changing the editor alone does not send the value.

19
00:01:29.175 --> 00:01:35.630
Now enter one hundred and send again. The receiver value and knob position

20
00:01:35.630 --> 00:01:40.596
change. Its four knobs show received settings: expression, volume, parameter

21
00:01:40.596 --> 00:01:46.058
A, and parameter B. These receiver knobs are read only indicators.

22
00:01:47.258 --> 00:01:52.180
The metal button below the knobs represents a footswitch. It is a read

23
00:01:52.180 --> 00:01:56.344
only indicator in this version. Send controller sixty four with value

24
00:01:56.344 --> 00:02:01.644
one hundred twenty seven to light its ring, or zero to turn it off.

25
00:02:01.644 --> 00:02:05.808
This is a test mapping, not a universal pedal bypass command.

26
00:02:07.008 --> 00:02:11.667
Select Program change, enter twenty four, and send. The reference display

27
00:02:11.667 --> 00:02:17.172
reads preset twenty five because it labels presets from one to one hundred

28
00:02:17.172 --> 00:02:22.254
twenty eight. MIDI program values on the wire start at zero. Always

29
00:02:22.254 --> 00:02:25.642
check the numbering used by the target device.

30
00:02:26.842 --> 00:02:31.931
Set the master clock to one hundred twenty beats per minute and press

31
00:02:31.931 --> 00:02:35.846
Start. The receiver shows Running and measures approximately one hundred

32
00:02:35.846 --> 00:02:40.935
twenty B P M from incoming clock ticks. Press Stop when finished. Physical

33
00:02:40.935 --> 00:02:44.850
equipment must support external MIDI clock for this to work.

34
00:02:46.050 --> 00:02:51.114
You have now tested keyboard input, control changes, a footswitch indicator,

35
00:02:51.114 --> 00:02:56.638
preset selection, and clock reception. MIDI OX can serve as a separate

36
00:02:56.638 --> 00:03:01.241
message inspector. These software loopback checks do not replace testing

37
00:03:01.241 --> 00:03:06.304
the actual musician hardware. Use the included written guide to repeat

38
00:03:06.304 --> 00:03:07.225
the demonstration.
